# SQL syntax error MariaDB server version for the right syntax to use near '@hotmail.com,Employee)' at line 1

When debugging a database insertion, seeing an error message like "right syntax to use near '@hotmail.com,Employee)" is often misleading if you look strictly at where the pointer lands. The parser has likely consumed characters before that point, but the specific token causing the failure is buried deeper in the query structure. In this scenario, the root cause isn't a typo in your `INSERT` clause; it is a fundamental mismatch between how you are constructing the SQL string and what the database engine expects when dealing with variables.

The provided code snippet attempts to execute a dynamic SQL command using `mysqli_prepare`. However, there is a critical flaw in the variable interpolation:

```php
$query = "INSERT INTO users(username, password,name,surname,email,role)VALUES($username, $password,$name,$lastname,$email,$role)";
$stmt = mysqli_prepare ($dbh, $query);
```

The code treats `$username`, `$password`, and other fields as literal strings within the SQL command. When a user inputs an email like `@hotmail.com` or a role like `Employee`, those values are inserted directly into the query string before it is parsed. This approach ignores the core security principle of prepared statements. If you intend to use placeholders (like `?`), you must never concatenate variables directly into the SQL text definition. Doing so renders the `mysqli_prepare` call effectively useless because the statement is already a completed, potentially vulnerable command.

The error message points to `@hotmail.com` because that value likely contains special characters or formatting that breaks the query logic when treated as raw text. For instance, if `$role` holds the value `Employee)`, and the SQL string ends up looking like `...VALUES(..., 'Employee)', ...`, the parser sees a mismatched parenthesis immediately after `VALUES`. The database engine tries to parse `)` as a closing delimiter for an open expression that doesn't exist in the context of the current statement structure.

To resolve this, you must switch to prepared statements with proper placeholders and bind parameters. This ensures that the database treats the input strictly as data, not executable code. Here is the corrected implementation using `mysqli_stmt_bind_param`:

```php
// Define the query with placeholders for all dynamic values
$sql = "INSERT INTO users (username, password, name, surname, email, role) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)";

$stmt = $dbh->prepare($sql);

// Determine types: 's' for string, 'i' for integer. Adjust based on your schema.
// Assuming all fields are strings for this example:
$types = "ssssss"; 

// Bind parameters
$stmt->bind_param($types, $username, $password, $name, $lastname, $email, $role);

// Execute the statement
$stmt->execute();
```

This approach handles special characters automatically. An email like `user@domain.com` or a name with apostrophes will be escaped correctly by the driver before reaching the server. It also prevents SQL injection attacks, which is impossible to guarantee with string concatenation.

If you are using Laravel, the framework abstracts these details away, handling binding and escaping internally via Eloquent queries or query builders. You can achieve the same safety without writing raw `bind_param` calls:

```php
User::create([
    'username' => $username,
    'password' => bcrypt($password),
    'name'     => $name,
    'surname'  => $lastname,
    'email'    => $email,
    'role'     => $role
]);
```

The trade-off here is a slight performance overhead due to the abstraction layer, but it eliminates the risk of syntax errors caused by manual string manipulation. Always verify that your column data types match the input you are sending; if `role` is an ENUM or integer in the database but you pass a string without proper mapping, similar parsing errors can occur even with prepared statements.

Ultimately, the error near `@hotmail.com` was a symptom of treating user input as code. By enforcing strict separation between SQL logic and data values through parameter binding, you ensure that MariaDB parses the statement structure correctly regardless of what characters appear in the input fields.
